/data/sda4 only exists inside the chroot. On the Android side the card is at /mnt/media_rw/45e4285f-…/AIbuild. Your quoting also split the command: the > file redirect ran as the shell user, not root, which is why it said Permission denied. Check that nothing stray was created on the internal /data, using rmdir, which only removes empty directories:

bash
adb shell "su -c 'rmdir /data/sda4/knowledge /data/sda4 2>&1; ls -ld /data/sda4 2>&1'"

Then write your note to the card, with your text unchanged. This overwrites the earlier arithmetic.txt:

bash
cat > /tmp/arithmetic.txt <<'EOF'
Arithmetic fast path rules:
Recognize direct arithmetic questions before knowledge retrieval or model inference.
Support addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, floor division, modulo, exponentiation, parentheses, negative numbers, decimals, and standard operator precedence.
Extract operands and operators deterministically and calculate in Python.
Never divide by zero; return Undefined: division by zero.
For recognized arithmetic, calculate and return Answer: result without invoking the model.
For unsupported expressions and word problems, continue through the normal knowledge and model pipeline.
EOF
adb push /tmp/arithmetic.txt /data/local/tmp/arithmetic.txt
adb shell "su -c 'mkdir -p /mnt/media_rw/45e4285f-5b14-40ea-89ca-ae6a59a14aa2/AIbuild/knowledge && cp /data/local/tmp/arithmetic.txt /mnt/media_rw/45e4285f-5b14-40ea-89ca-ae6a59a14aa2/AIbuild/knowledge/arithmetic.txt && cat /mnt/media_rw/45e4285f-5b14-40ea-89ca-ae6a59a14aa2/AIbuild/knowledge/arithmetic.txt'"

That note is only reference text. The model reads it and nothing more. The calculation happens in code, and the code block I gave last time supports fewer features than your note lists (no parentheses, decimals, modulo, powers or precedence). This replacement implements every rule in the note using a safe ast evaluator, with no eval. It works on your /tmp/llm.new and uses patterns, not line numbers.

Remove the earlier block if it was inserted. This deletes whatever sits between def model_chat and messages = kb_wrap(messages), and does nothing if there’s nothing there:

bash
sed -i '/^def model_chat/,/^    messages = kb_wrap(messages)/{/^def model_chat/!{/^    messages = kb_wrap(messages)/!d}}' /tmp/llm.new
for m in re ast operator; do grep -q "^import $m\$" /tmp/llm.new || sed -i "/^import os\$/a import $m" /tmp/llm.new; done

Write the helper functions and the hook:

bash
cat > /tmp/arith_funcs.py <<'EOF'

_ARITH_OPS = {
    ast.Add: operator.add, ast.Sub: operator.sub, ast.Mult: operator.mul,
    ast.Div: operator.truediv, ast.FloorDiv: operator.floordiv,
    ast.Mod: operator.mod, ast.Pow: operator.pow,
    ast.USub: operator.neg, ast.UAdd: operator.pos,
}
_ARITH_WORDS = [
    (r"\bto the power of\b", "**"), (r"\bdivided by\b", "/"),
    (r"\bmultiplied by\b", "*"), (r"\btimes\b", "*"),
    (r"\bplus\b", "+"), (r"\bminus\b", "-"), (r"\bmod(?:ulo)?\b", "%"),
]


def _arith_eval(node):
    if isinstance(node, ast.Expression):
        return _arith_eval(node.body)
    if isinstance(node, ast.Constant) and type(node.value) in (int, float):
        return node.value
    if isinstance(node, ast.BinOp) and type(node.op) in _ARITH_OPS:
        left, right = _arith_eval(node.left), _arith_eval(node.right)
        if isinstance(node.op, ast.Pow) and (abs(right) > 1000 or abs(left) > 10 ** 6):
            raise ValueError("too large")
        return _ARITH_OPS[type(node.op)](left, right)
    if isinstance(node, ast.UnaryOp) and type(node.op) in _ARITH_OPS:
        return _ARITH_OPS[type(node.op)](_arith_eval(node.operand))
    raise ValueError("unsupported")


def arith_fast_path(text):
    """Return the answer for direct arithmetic, or None to use the model."""
    m = re.match(r"(?i)^\s*(?:what is|what's|calculate|compute|evaluate)\s+(.+)$", text.strip())
    if not m:
        return None
    t = m.group(1).rstrip("?=. ").strip()
    for pat, rep in _ARITH_WORDS:
        t = re.sub(pat, rep, t, flags=re.I)
    t = re.sub(r"(?<=\d)\s*x\s*(?=\d)", "*", t, flags=re.I)
    t = t.replace("^", "**").replace(",", "")
    if not re.fullmatch(r"[\d\s+\-*/%().]+", t) or not re.search(r"\d", t) or not re.search(r"[+\-*/%]", t):
        return None
    try:
        val = _arith_eval(ast.parse(t, mode="eval"))
    except ZeroDivisionError:
        return "Undefined: division by zero."
    except Exception:
        return None
    if isinstance(val, float):
        val = int(val) if val.is_integer() else float(format(val, ".12g"))
    return f"{t} = {val}\nAnswer: {val}"
EOF
cat > /tmp/arith_hook.py <<'EOF'
    _fast = arith_fast_path(messages[-1]["content"])
    if _fast is not None:
        sys.stdout.write(_fast + "\n")
        sys.stdout.flush()
        return _fast
EOF
sed -i '/^URL = /r /tmp/arith_funcs.py' /tmp/llm.new
sed -i '/^def model_chat/r /tmp/arith_hook.py' /tmp/llm.new
grep -n -E "^import|^URL|^def arith_fast_path|^def model_chat|arith_fast_path\(messages" /tmp/llm.new
sed -n '/^def model_chat/,/messages = kb_wrap/p' /tmp/llm.new

In the grep output, you should see import re, import ast and import operator exactly once each, def arith_fast_path once, and the arith_fast_path(messages call once. The sed -n should show the hook directly under the model_chat header, then messages = kb_wrap(messages). If any line appears twice, an earlier insert is still there, so paste the grep output instead of installing.

Install, syntax-check, and test the function directly without touching the model:

bash
adb push /tmp/llm.new /data/local/tmp/llm.new
adb shell "su -c 'cp -n /data/local/nhsystem/kali-arm64/usr/local/bin/llm /data/local/nhsystem/kali-arm64/usr/local/bin/llm.bak; cp /data/local/tmp/llm.new /data/local/nhsystem/kali-arm64/usr/local/bin/llm && chmod 755 /data/local/nhsystem/kali-arm64/usr/local/bin/llm'"
adb shell "su -c '$C python3 -m py_compile /usr/local/bin/llm && echo SYNTAX_OK'"

Then open llm 2 and try these: what is 38 times 46 (1748), what is (12+8)*3^2 (180), what is 7 / 2 (3.5), what is 5 / 0 (the division-by-zero message). Each should answer instantly with no model load. what is the capital of France should still go to the model, because it doesn’t match the arithmetic pattern.

The fast path only fires on messages starting with what is, what's, calculate, compute or evaluate. A bare 12*7 goes to the model. Say the word if you want bare expressions caught too. The cost is false positives on things like dates (2024-10-10 would evaluate to 2004).
